# paw

**12 small React components for everyday interfaces.**

Use paw for the basics: buttons, form fields, layout, and feedback. Import what you need and style it to match your app.

## Install

```sh
npm install paw
```

## Quick start

```jsx
import { Button, Input, Stack, Text } from "paw";

export default function NewsletterForm({ onSubscribe }) {
  function handleSubmit(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    const email = new FormData(event.currentTarget).get("email");
    onSubscribe(email);
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <Stack>
        <Text>Get one email when we publish something new.</Text>

        <label htmlFor="newsletter-email">Email address</label>
        <Input
          id="newsletter-email"
          name="email"
          type="email"
          autoComplete="email"
          required
        />

        <Button type="submit">Subscribe</Button>
      </Stack>
    </form>
  );
}
```

## Components

| Component | Use it for |
| --- | --- |
| `Box` | Grouping content in a layout container |
| `Stack` | Arranging elements in a row or column |
| `Text` | Rendering body copy and short descriptions |
| `Button` | Submitting forms or triggering actions |
| `Input` | Entering a single line of text |
| `Textarea` | Entering longer, multiline text |
| `Checkbox` | Selecting an independent option |
| `Radio` | Selecting one option from a group |
| `Badge` | Displaying a short status or category |
| `Divider` | Separating related sections |
| `Spinner` | Indicating that work is in progress |
| `Dialog` | Presenting a task or decision in a modal |

## Styling

Give components a `className` to use your app’s styles.

```jsx
<Button className="save-button" type="submit">
  Save changes
</Button>
```

```css
.save-button {
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
  border: 1px solid transparent;
  border-radius: 0.5rem;
  background: #2448c5;
  color: white;
}

.save-button:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid #2448c5;
  outline-offset: 3px;
}
```

## Accessibility

Pair form controls with visible labels, give dialogs a descriptive title, and don’t rely on color alone to communicate status. When a spinner is the only visual feedback, include a text status such as “Saving changes…” that assistive technology can announce.

# paw

**12 small React primitives for building interfaces that look like yours.**

paw gives you the building blocks for forms, feedback, and overlays without prescribing a design system. Use the components on their own or compose them into patterns your app can reuse.

## Installation

```sh
npm install paw
```

## Quick start

```jsx
import { Button, Input, Label, Stack } from 'paw';

export default function NewsletterForm() {
  return (
    <form
      onSubmit={(event) => {
        event.preventDefault();
        const email = new FormData(event.currentTarget).get('email');
        // Send the email address to your signup endpoint.
      }}
    >
      <Stack>
        <Label htmlFor="newsletter-email">Email address</Label>
        <Input
          id="newsletter-email"
          name="email"
          type="email"
          autoComplete="email"
          required
        />
        <Button type="submit">Subscribe</Button>
      </Stack>
    </form>
  );
}
```

## The 12 primitives

| Primitive | Use it for |
| --- | --- |
| `Box` | A generic wrapper for grouping and styling content. |
| `Stack` | Arranging elements in a vertical group. |
| `Text` | Rendering copy and inline text. |
| `Button` | Triggering an action or submitting a form. |
| `Input` | Collecting a single line of text. |
| `Label` | Giving a form control a visible, accessible name. |
| `Checkbox` | Turning an option on or off. |
| `Switch` | Toggling a setting that takes effect immediately. |
| `Badge` | Displaying a short status or category. |
| `Separator` | Dividing related sections of content. |
| `Dialog` | Presenting a task or decision in a modal. |
| `Tooltip` | Providing a brief hint on hover or keyboard focus. |

## Styling

Use your app’s CSS to define colors, spacing, and typography. Start with a local class and reuse it wherever you need the same treatment:

```jsx
<Button className="primary-button" type="button">
  Save changes
</Button>
```

```css
.primary-button {
  padding: 0.5rem 0.875rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 0.5rem;
  background: #4338ca;
  color: #fff;
  font: inherit;
}

.primary-button:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid #4338ca;
  outline-offset: 3px;
}
```

## Accessibility

Pair form controls with visible labels, give icon-only buttons accessible names, and preserve keyboard focus indicators when adding custom styles. Use tooltips for supplementary hints, not for instructions people need to complete a task. When composing a dialog, test the full keyboard flow: opening it, moving between controls, closing it, and returning focus to the trigger.

